Package: dports
Version: 2.6.0
Architecture: all
Maintainer: JonPark0 <yeochoony@gmail.com>
Depends: bash (>= 4.3)
Suggests: docker-ce-cli | docker.io | docker-cli | podman | incus-client, docker-compose-plugin | docker-compose-v2 | podman-compose, skopeo, docker-buildx-plugin | docker-buildx, jq | python3, curl | wget
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://git.palnarium.com/JonPark0/dports
Description: container port mapping display tool (Docker, Podman, Incus, LXD)
 dports displays the port mappings of Docker and Podman containers and of
 Incus and LXD instances (proxy devices, network forwards) in a clean table.
 It replaces the need to parse verbose docker ps output by showing only the
 port, container name, and image in a readable format.
 .
 Features:
  - Colored tabular output with automatic terminal detection
  - Filter by port number, container name, or image name
  - Verbose mode showing internal port mappings
  - JSON output for scripting and automation
  - Quiet mode (port numbers only)
  - Support for stopped containers
  - Cloudflare Tunnel routes: hostnames served by cloudflared and the
    containers behind them
  - "dports update": check running containers' images for updates, pull
    them and recreate the Compose services that use them, in one
    full-screen flow (or report only, or do it all without asking)
  - "dports -I" (experimental): browse ports and tunnel routes, with search,
    container details, logs, restart and shell
  - Completions for Fish and Zsh
  - Engines are auto-detected without contacting any daemon, or chosen in
    /etc/dports.conf or ~/.config/dports/config
